[s0l0!-header][h1]Reading from a File[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

The [b]stdio.h[/b] library also includes functions for reading from an open file. A file can be read one character at a time or an entire string can be read into a character [b]buffer[/b], which is typically a char array used for temporary storage.

[b]fgetc(fp)[/b] Returns the next character from the file pointed to by [i]fp[/i]. If the end of the file has been reached, then [b]EOF[/b] is returned.

[b]fgets(buff, n, fp)[/b] Reads n-1 characters from the file pointed to by [i]fp[/i] and stores the string in buff. A NULL character '\0' is appended as the last character in [i]buff[/i]. If fgets encounters a newline character or the end of file before n-1 characters is reached, then only the characters up to that point are stored in buff.

[b]fscanf(fp, conversion_specifiers, vars)[/b] Reads characters from the file pointed to by [i]fp[/i] and assigns input to a list of variable pointers [i]vars[/i] using [i]conversion_specifiers[/i]. As with scanf, fscanf stops reading a string when a space or newline is encountered.

The following program demonstrates reading from a file:
[code format="c" codeId="2170"]#include <stdio.h>

int main() {  
[b]  FILE *fptr;[/b]
  int c, stock;
  char buffer[200], item[10];
  float price;

  /* myfile.txt: Inventory\n100 Widget 0.29\nEnd of List */

  [b]fptr = fopen("myfile.txt", "r");[/b]

  [b]fgets(buffer, 20, fptr);[/b]    /* read a line */
  printf("%s\n", buffer);

  [b]fscanf[/b]([b]fptr[/b], "%d%s%f", &stock, item, &price); /* read data */
  printf("%d  %s  %4.2f\n", stock, item, price);

  while ([b](c = getc(fptr)) != EOF[/b]) /* read the rest of the file */
    printf("%c", c);

  fclose(fptr);
  return 0;
} [/code]
The [b]gets()[/b] function reads up until the newline. [b]fscanf()[/b] reads data according to conversion specifiers. And then the while loop reads one character at a time until the end of file. Checking for a problem when opening the file (a NULL pointer) was left out to shorten the example.
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

